\chapter{Numerical Methods}

\section{Finite Difference Operators}

Spatial derivatives are discretized using fourth-order centered finite
difference stencils. For a function $f$ defined on a uniform grid, the
first derivative in the $x$-direction is approximated by:
\begin{equation}
    \partial_x f_i =
    \frac{-f_{i+2} + 8f_{i+1} - 8f_{i-1} + f_{i-2}}{12\Delta x}.
\end{equation}

Second derivatives follow:
\begin{equation}
    \partial_{xx} f_i =
    \frac{-f_{i+2} + 16f_{i+1} - 30f_i + 16f_{i-1} - f_{i-2}}{12\Delta x^2}.
\end{equation}

These operators are applied independently on each AMR block, ensuring
local consistency and minimizing inter-block communication.

\section{Runge--Kutta Time Integration}

Time evolution uses the classical fourth-order Runge--Kutta (RK4)
integrator:
\begin{align}
    k_1 &= F(Q^n), \\
    k_2 &= F(Q^n + \frac{\Delta t}{2}k_1), \\
    k_3 &= F(Q^n + \frac{\Delta t}{2}k_2), \\
    k_4 &= F(Q^n + \Delta t\,k_3),
\end{align}
with the update:
\begin{equation}
    Q^{n+1} = Q^n + \frac{\Delta t}{6}(k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4).
\end{equation}

The timestep is constrained by the Courant condition:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta t \le C \min_{\ell}(\Delta x_\ell),
\end{equation}
with $C = 0.25$ for stability across refinement levels.

\section{GPU-Resident AMR}

The AMR hierarchy consists of seven refinement levels, each represented as
a uniform Cartesian grid. Refinement is triggered by curvature-based
criteria:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{C}(x) = |R(x)| + |K(x)|,
\end{equation}
and by apparent horizon detection using the null-expansion scalar:
\begin{equation}
    \theta \le 10^{-3}.
\end{equation}

All AMR operations are executed on NVIDIA GPUs. Each refinement level is
assigned to a GPU kernel group, and no CPU-side refinement logic is used.

\section{Boundary Kernels}

Boundary operations are performed using three CUDA kernels:

\subsection{Prolongation}
\begin{equation}
    Q^{\ell+1} = \mathcal{P}(Q^\ell),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{P}$ interpolates coarse-level data to fine-level grids.

\subsection{Restriction}
\begin{equation}
    Q^\ell = \mathcal{R}(Q^{\ell+1}),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}$ averages fine-level data back to coarse grids.

\subsection{Flux Synchronization}
\begin{equation}
    F^\ell = \mathcal{S}(F^{\ell+1}),
\end{equation}
ensuring conservation across refinement boundaries.

All kernels operate elementwise and avoid warp divergence by using
uniform stencil patterns.

\section{Constraint Homeostasis Controller}

The homeostasis controller monitors ADM constraints:
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{H} &= R + K^2 - K_{ij}K^{ij}, \\
    \mathcal{M}^i &= D_j(K^{ij} - \gamma^{ij}K).
\end{align}

Global norms are computed each timestep:
\begin{equation}
    \|\mathcal{H}\|_2 = 
    \left( \sum_x \mathcal{H}(x)^2 \Delta V \right)^{1/2},
\end{equation}
and similarly for $\|\mathcal{M}\|_2$.

The controller enforces:
\begin{equation}
    \|\mathcal{H}\|_2, \|\mathcal{M}\|_2 \le 1.21 \times 10^{-13}.
\end{equation}

If constraints rise above threshold, the controller:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item increases local refinement,
    \item adjusts gauge damping parameter $\eta$,
    \item reduces timestep $\Delta t$,
    \item applies localized dissipation filters.
\end{enumerate}

This ensures long-duration stability across strong-field and
semiclassical regimes.

\section{GPU Memory Layout}

Each AMR block stores:
\begin{itemize}
    \item evolved variables ($\tilde{\gamma}_{ij}$, $\phi$, $\tilde{A}_{ij}$, $K$, $\tilde{\Gamma}^i$),
    \item gauge fields ($\alpha$, $\beta^i$, $B^i$),
    \item curvature invariants ($R$, $I$, $J$),
    \item constraint buffers ($\mathcal{H}$, $\mathcal{M}^i$).
\end{itemize}

Memory is arranged in structure-of-arrays (SoA) format for coalesced
access.

\section{Inter-Block Synchronization}

Synchronization uses:

\begin{itemize}
    \item ghost-zone population,
    \item flux matching,
    \item prolongation/restriction consistency checks,
    \item non-blocking MPI communication for multi-node runs.
\end{itemize}

This ensures global consistency across the AMR hierarchy.